      "title": "PDF Security in Real Estate: Protecting Client Data Before a Transaction",
      "desc": "Targets agents, notaries, and brokers sharing documents with personal and financial data.",
      "body": "<p>When real estate agencies, notaries, and mortgage brokers work with contracts, identity copies, extracts, plans, and property documents, the risk is not only losing a file. The real risk is sending a document that looks correct on screen while still carrying internal information the recipient should never see.</p>\n<p>The most common issues come from exposed personal data, wrong versions sent to clients, and PDFs that are hard to verify. These elements are not always visible in a normal viewer, but they can matter in an audit, a dispute, a GDPR review, or a client-facing delivery.</p>\n<p>DocInspector is positioned as a Windows desktop application that processes documents locally. In this scenario, its core value is straightforward: teams can run metadata cleaning, watermarking, and conversion into a coherent package across hundreds or thousands of files without uploading them to a cloud service and without breaking the original folder structure.</p>\n<p>Practical checklist:</p>\n<ul><li>Run a local audit on the full folder, not only on the files you plan to send.</li><li>Check the main risks: exposed personal data, wrong versions sent to clients, and PDFs that are hard to verify.</li><li>Apply metadata cleaning, watermarking, and conversion into a coherent package on a working copy, then preserve the original separately.</li><li>Generate an Excel report or audit trail as internal evidence of the workflow.</li><li>Preserve the folder structure so the operational context of the documents is not lost.</li></ul><p>Recommended workflow: create a working copy of the folder, run analysis across the full structure, identify problematic documents, apply the required operations, and export results into a separate output folder. For teams, the final report matters as much as the cleaned file because it documents what was processed and where issues were found.</p>\n<p>For SEO, the article can naturally target phrases such as: real estate PDF security, property document protection, client data protection, transaction documents, PDF watermark. These phrases should appear in subheadings, the meta description, the main image filename, and the first paragraph of the page.</p>\n<p>Bottom line: professional documents should be treated as sensitive assets, not ordinary attachments. A local audit before sharing reduces operational risk, increases client trust, and turns document preparation into a repeatable process.</p>\n"
    },
