      "desc": "Content for financial workflows with thousands of documents and traceability requirements.",
      "body": "<p>When accountants, financial auditors, and tax consultants work with invoices, registers, reports, spreadsheets, and supporting PDFs, the risk is not only losing a file. The real risk is sending a document that looks correct on screen while still carrying internal information the recipient should never see.</p>\n<p>The most common issues come from duplicate files, manual page counts, missing indexes, and exposure of financial data. These elements are not always visible in a normal viewer, but they can matter in an audit, a dispute, a GDPR review, or a client-facing delivery.</p>\n<p>DocInspector is positioned as a Windows desktop application that processes documents locally. In this scenario, its core value is straightforward: teams can run Excel reporting, page counts, and metadata audit across hundreds or thousands of files without uploading them to a cloud service and without breaking the original folder structure.</p>\n<p>Practical checklist:</p>\n<ul><li>Run a local audit on the full folder, not only on the files you plan to send.</li><li>Check the main risks: duplicate files, manual page counts, missing indexes, and exposure of financial data.</li><li>Apply Excel reporting, page counts, and metadata audit on a working copy, then preserve the original separately.</li><li>Generate an Excel report or audit trail as internal evidence of the workflow.</li><li>Preserve the folder structure so the operational context of the documents is not lost.</li></ul><p>Recommended workflow: create a working copy of the folder, run analysis across the full structure, identify problematic documents, apply the required operations, and export results into a separate output folder. For teams, the final report matters as much as the cleaned file because it documents what was processed and where issues were found.</p>\n<p>For SEO, the article can naturally target phrases such as: accounting document management, financial document audit, PDF Excel report, document hashes, invoice metadata. These phrases should appear in subheadings, the meta description, the main image filename, and the first paragraph of the page.</p>\n<p>Bottom line: professional documents should be treated as sensitive assets, not ordinary attachments. A local audit before sharing reduces operational risk, increases client trust, and turns document preparation into a repeatable process.</p>\n"
