    "en": {
      "title": "M&A Data Room Preparation: Clean Documents Before Investors See Them",
      "desc": "An article for founders, consultants, and finance teams preparing documents for due diligence.",
      "body": "<p>When founders, CFOs, M&A consultants, and legal teams work with contracts, cap tables, financial reports, internal policies, and IP documents, the risk is not only losing a file. The real risk is sending a document that looks correct on screen while still carrying internal information the recipient should never see.</p>\n<p>The most common issues come from strategic metadata, internal comments, messy folders, and documents that look unprepared. These elements are not always visible in a normal viewer, but they can matter in an audit, a dispute, a GDPR review, or a client-facing delivery.</p>\n<p>DocInspector is positioned as a Windows desktop application that processes documents locally. In this scenario, its core value is straightforward: teams can run local audit, cleaning, and evidence-report structure across hundreds or thousands of files without uploading them to a cloud service and without breaking the original folder structure.</p>\n<p>Practical checklist:</p>\n<ul><li>Run a local audit on the full folder, not only on the files you plan to send.</li><li>Check the main risks: strategic metadata, internal comments, messy folders, and documents that look unprepared.</li><li>Apply local audit, cleaning, and evidence-report structure on a working copy, then preserve the original separately.</li><li>Generate an Excel report or audit trail as internal evidence of the workflow.</li><li>Preserve the folder structure so the operational context of the documents is not lost.</li></ul><p>Recommended workflow: create a working copy of the folder, run analysis across the full structure, identify problematic documents, apply the required operations, and export results into a separate output folder. For teams, the final report matters as much as the cleaned file because it documents what was processed and where issues were found.</p>\n<p>For SEO, the article can naturally target phrases such as: M&A data room, due diligence documents, metadata cleaning, data room audit, investor preparation. These phrases should appear in subheadings, the meta description, the main image filename, and the first paragraph of the page.</p>\n<p>Bottom line: professional documents should be treated as sensitive assets, not ordinary attachments. A local audit before sharing reduces operational risk, increases client trust, and turns document preparation into a repeatable process.</p>\n<p>EDUCATION</p>\n<p>Articole educaționale pentru căutări informaționale și long-tail SEO. / Образовательные статьи для информационных запросов и long-tail SEO. / Educational articles for informational search intent and long-tail SEO.</p>\n"
    },
