      "desc": "Explains the value of a report with file names, sizes, pages, metadata, and hashes.",
      "body": "<p>When project managers, lawyers, auditors, and document administrators work with work archives, client files, and complex structures with many subfolders, the risk is not only losing a file. The real risk is sending a document that looks correct on screen while still carrying internal information the recipient should never see.</p>\n<p>The most common issues come from lack of visibility, manual calculations, hidden files, and assumption-based decisions. These elements are not always visible in a normal viewer, but they can matter in an audit, a dispute, a GDPR review, or a client-facing delivery.</p>\n<p>DocInspector is positioned as a Windows desktop application that processes documents locally. In this scenario, its core value is straightforward: teams can run folder evidence report exported to XLSX across hundreds or thousands of files without uploading them to a cloud service and without breaking the original folder structure.</p>\n<p>Practical checklist:</p>\n<ul><li>Run a local audit on the full folder, not only on the files you plan to send.</li><li>Check the main risks: lack of visibility, manual calculations, hidden files, and assumption-based decisions.</li><li>Apply folder evidence report exported to XLSX on a working copy, then preserve the original separately.</li><li>Generate an Excel report or audit trail as internal evidence of the workflow.</li><li>Preserve the folder structure so the operational context of the documents is not lost.</li></ul><p>Recommended workflow: create a working copy of the folder, run analysis across the full structure, identify problematic documents, apply the required operations, and export results into a separate output folder. For teams, the final report matters as much as the cleaned file because it documents what was processed and where issues were found.</p>\n<p>For SEO, the article can naturally target phrases such as: Excel document report, folder audit, XLSX document inventory, PDF page count, file hashes. These phrases should appear in subheadings, the meta description, the main image filename, and the first paragraph of the page.</p>\n<p>Bottom line: professional documents should be treated as sensitive assets, not ordinary attachments. A local audit before sharing reduces operational risk, increases client trust, and turns document preparation into a repeatable process.</p>\n"
    },
